"""Community health risk literacy and referral coordination."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(100, 'Community Health Risks and Referral Coordination',
        'Community Health\nRisks and Referral\nCoordination',
        'Coordinate safe, confidential support after a reported extractive-related concern and communicate evidence and decision boundaries.', [
        module('Distinguish a hazard report from a health finding',
            'Structure a reported concern by possible pathway, evidence status and referral authority.',
            """A hazard is a possible source of harm. Exposure concerns whether people actually come into contact with it and in what circumstances. Health impacts depend on more than the presence of a hazard. An unusual smell, a reported symptom and a measured environmental concentration are distinct observations. A community practitioner can document when and where a concern was reported, who is potentially affected in aggregate, and the route through which the concern might arise. Record hypotheses as hypotheses and refer risk assessment to the qualified team. [S85]

Research can explain why a report warrants attention without determining what happened to an individual. Bruederle and Hodler linked historical spill and survey records and compared siblings conceived before and after nearby spill events. The analysis supports a concern about neonatal risk in the study context and depends on assumptions about exposure and comparison. It cannot diagnose a resident or identify a current contaminant from a description. State the study’s population, place and design when using any finding in a briefing. [R94]

The 2011 Ogoniland assessment combined community engagement, professional sampling and examination of other records in a specific place and period. It illustrates why it matters to record the source and time of evidence. In a fictional case, note separately what residents heard, what a project report said and what a qualified authority has verified. Direct current safety questions to that authority. Use the supplied records without attempting physical inspection, entry or collection. [S90]

In a first contact, check what support the person is asking for and agree a safe follow-up. Record a time-sensitive referral promptly when indicated by authorised protocols. A professional assessment and practical support can proceed while the environmental question is investigated.""",
            'Residents report a smell near a shared supply after a works change. One speaker reports illness and asks the practitioner to certify the cause.',
            'Prepare a three-column record for observations, uncertainties and responsible referrals. State what the practitioner can tell the resident.',
            'Recognise the concern, separate source and symptom reports, and refer site evidence and clinical assessment to the relevant qualified authorities.',
            """
Identify exposure information. | A resident reports a smell from an unknown location. Which detail best helps the qualified team evaluate possible contact? | Place, time and circumstances as reported. | A diagnosis coded before referral. | An estimate of the chemical identity based on smell alone. | A declaration that every resident was exposed. | An observed or reported context supports qualified follow-up.
Read study design. | What distinguishes the Nigerian PNAS study from an individual clinical evaluation? | It compared linked historical observations among siblings and estimated a group effect. | It tested each classroom case at the same site. | It identified current contaminants in every settlement. | It verified the health outcome of a specific new patient. | The research design answers a population question.
Label historical evidence. | What does the 2011 Ogoniland assessment establish for a new fictional concern? | How multiple evidence streams can inform a documented study, with historical limits. | Current conditions for an unassessed site. | A medical history for a new resident. | A permanent clearance after sampling ended. | Historical findings have defined place and time.
Separate reported symptoms. | How should a symptom claim appear in the initial community note? | Attribute it to the report and refer clinical assessment. | Confirm the same symptom for everyone who lives nearby. | Give it a technical cause before assessment. | Omit it solely because sampling has not happened. | Respect the report and retain the distinction between testimony and diagnosis.
Use parallel support. | An environmental investigation may take time and a household requests support now. What is appropriate? | Arrange appropriate support and follow up on the separate environmental referral. | Wait for the environmental cause before receiving any support request. | Issue a technical finding to accelerate the referral. | Share the full case record with every party. | Support can proceed within role while facts are investigated.
"""),
        module('Build a referral that reaches the receiving service',
            'Design a consent-aware referral, a safe follow-up and a way to measure access barriers.',
            """A referral connects a person with a service that can respond within its mandate. Ask for the person’s priorities and confirm the route is usable, including appointment arrangements, language, cost, transport and safe contact details. Explain the purpose and what data must be shared. Obtain and record agreement through the applicable process and explain relevant limits. In the classroom, use fictional identifiers. A request from a funder or project liaison does not by itself authorise a transfer of personal records. [S85]

Structure the handover so the recipient can act. Provide the minimum relevant facts, the agreed method of contact, any access arrangements, and timing agreed with the person. Follow existing professional protocols for urgent needs. Ask the receiving service whether the referral arrived and can be accepted. If it cannot, record a permitted fallback route. Keep identity information separate from aggregate summaries used to improve service access. These steps reduce unnecessary disclosure while making a lost or incomplete referral easier to detect. [S85]

Experience in Emohua, discussed in the secondary study by Mezewo and Emeodu, links social work with service education, psychosocial support and advocacy. That discussion can help a team think about multiple forms of response. It does not show that a referral method works in a particular clinic. Compare the route against the current service’s stated access rules and check outcomes with the person. [R97]

Use stage counts to understand what happens. Of 20 consented referrals, 16 accepted by the receiving agency and 12 accessed represent different progress measures. Look for delays at each step. If a person could not travel, ask what would help and whether the service can arrange a suitable alternative. Schedule a follow-up at a mutually safe time.""",
            'From 20 consented referrals, 16 are accepted and 12 people reach the clinic. Several cannot pay for travel or use the default callback number safely.',
            'Calculate accepted and completed rates using named denominators. Set a safe contact option and an agreed referral fallback.',
            'Report 80 percent accepted and 60 percent accessed of all referrals. Confirm actual access, record consent and offer a practical fallback for travel and contact.',
            """
Check referral usability. | A service is open but has no accessible appointment slot for the person. Which response best handles the barrier? | Verify an alternative slot or service within the agreed referral process. | Count the referral as completed immediately. | Tell everyone to use the same slot. | Send case details to all alternative services at once. | Opening hours do not guarantee someone can access an appointment.
Limit transfer. | An operator funding transport requests named clinic histories. What should the practitioner share to discuss cost in general? | Relevant aggregate travel barriers. | The named histories requested. | Clinical notes with only page numbers removed. | A dataset of everyone referred. | Aggregate barriers can support planning without excessive disclosure.
Measure completion. | Twelve of 20 people reached the clinic. What percentage accessed it from the referral cohort? | 60 percent. | 75 percent. | 80 percent. | 20 percent. | Twelve divided by twenty is sixty percent.
Confirm receipt separately. | A secure referral was sent and the clinic has not confirmed receipt. What should the practitioner do? | Check receipt through the agreed route and record the result. | Mark it accepted because it was sent. | Publish the issue with case names to prompt action. | Assume the person rejected help. | Sending and receiving are distinct stages.
Use agreed contact. | A household says calls to its usual number could create harm. How should a follow-up be arranged? | Agree a safer method and time and record only what is needed. | Call repeatedly at the same number. | Post the appointment publicly. | Ask the project manager to describe the person’s concern publicly. | A safe contact method is part of effective follow-up.
"""),
        module('Communicate uncertainty and verify service continuity',
            'Prepare a clear community update and a plan for reviewing equitable service continuity.',
            """An update after a reported hazard should distinguish confirmed information, reported experience and the next action. Say what period and place the update covers, who is responsible for the current advice and when it will be updated. Explain confirmed findings using the authority’s current wording or an approved plain-language explanation. Avoid issuing site clearance, identifying a likely contaminant from appearance or stating that a specific illness was caused by an unverified event. Provide authorised contact routes that people can use. [S90]

Use an accessible form that reaches the groups concerned. A written notice can be supplemented with audio, interpreted communication or trusted intermediaries if appropriate. Test whether people understood the practical step without requiring them to identify themselves publicly. For private support routes, agree how messages are returned and which personal details are essential. When correcting an earlier update, say what changed and why. Retain an accurate archive for review and prevent the outdated version from circulating as new advice. [S85]

Follow access as well as communication. Compare referrals offered, service reached and barriers resolved at agreed times. Check people who depend on different routes rather than relying on a single group with reliable phone access. Handle public results so small groups and sensitive case details cannot be identified. If clinic access worsens during works, identify another service or travel arrangement through responsible bodies and check that it is operating. [R97]

Keep an explicit review decision. For each unresolved condition, write who can investigate, what evidence is expected and who will communicate the next update. The delay itself may justify a service improvement. A repeated barrier can be addressed without overstating the technical cause of the underlying concern.""",
            'A project text labels concern reports resolved because a notice was sent. People without phones do not know when the mobile clinic runs.',
            'Write a four-sentence update with a date, evidence status, action owner and accessible contact. Propose one continuity check.',
            'Date and scope the update. Explain the confirmed service route and alternate contact, preserve uncertainty and verify actual access for those missed.',
            """
Bound the message. | Which statement belongs in a public update based on a qualified report for a specified site last week? | State the report’s site, date and decision scope. | Apply it to all nearby settlements for the whole year. | Omit the date to keep the message short. | Describe current conditions everywhere as identical. | Scope prevents stale or place-specific evidence being overextended.
Verify comprehension. | A notice reached many people but few know the next service step. Which evidence is useful? | An accessible check that people can state or locate the next step. | The number of messages sent. | The length of the notice. | The number of managers who approved its tone. | Receipt and comprehension are different stages.
Correct old updates. | The clinic schedule changes after an earlier notice. What should be communicated? | The revised date, replacement of the earlier notice and a contact route. | Only the old notice for consistency. | A new notice with no effective date. | Individual reasons for every missed appointment. | Version clarity reduces confusion and unnecessary disclosure.
Test continuity. | Which indicator best shows that a mobile clinic alternative serves people affected by a road closure? | Share of the relevant referred group who actually reach the alternative. | The number of notices printed. | The length of the planned clinic visit. | The number of programme partners named. | Measure actual service access for the affected group.
Assign follow-up. | The qualified team has not confirmed a reported environmental finding. What should the case update include? | The unresolved question, competent reviewer and next update time. | A definite cause chosen by community vote. | A claim of clearance based on silence. | A promise of a technical decision from the social worker. | Named owners and review times sustain accountability while evidence is pending.
"""),
    ])
